{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794137384,"nonce":"U9Ho+2BSDS3ZkEAz58BqgSP5gog=","message":"Backend policy, refining the order: wgpu stays where it absorbs known driver trouble, and a native backend replaces it only on platforms without that trouble. The tracker supports it. GPU or driver bugs cluster on Linux (e17d13a Wayland swapchain starvation, d6037ec the wgpu-hal 26.0.6 Mesa and NVIDIA acquire regression), Android (4e110cb resume goes black; PowerVR and Tensor G5 quirks noted in the workspace manifest) and web (4477494 WebGL2 fallback). None is tracked for Apple GPUs, and none for Windows DX12, which may only mean it is less tested there. So: Apple (macOS and iOS) native Metal first (310506c); Linux, Android and the browser stay on wgpu until a measured need; Windows is undecided and should wait for Metal to prove the interface (GPUI's Windows renderer is custom, per the Zed coverage, so there is precedent). Web is a separate question: the browser already is the driver layer, and wgpu plus naga inside the wasm bundle is download weight, so a thin direct WebGPU and WebGL2 backend fed by precompiled shaders may pay off for size. Measure the wasm cost before deciding. Conditions: backends chosen by target in Cargo so an Apple build contains no wgpu or naga; one shared conformance suite (the existing goldens) run against every backend; shaders compiled once by the offline pipeline so backends cannot drift; the narrow interface stays strict, or two renderers become two products.","files":null}]}